WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.463
Federal AI policy was rewritten in 2025.

2
00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:07.216
One OMB memo governs use,
with a Chief AI Officer,

3
00:00:07.216 --> 00:00:12.800
an annual use-case inventory, and
minimum practices for high-impact AI.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.188
Its companion governs acquisition,
and says AI run as an information

5
00:00:17.188 --> 00:00:21.038
system needs an authorization
to operate before it deploys.

6
00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:23.676
What the rules don't do
matters just as much.

7
00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:26.375
There's no AI-specific authorization.

8
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:31.069
Agencies keep following their existing
authorization process, the memo's own

9
00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:35.762
risk acceptance doesn't replace it, and
it sets no identity requirements at all.

10
00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:39.493
So a high-impact use case needs
two decisions before it deploys:

11
00:00:39.493 --> 00:00:44.513
the signed risk acceptance on its impact
assessment, and the system's authorization.

12
00:00:44.513 --> 00:00:49.796
One footnote: a recent executive order
changes the preferred term in some documents,

13
00:00:49.796 --> 00:00:54.400
but this book keeps saying AI, because
the instruments it applies still do.

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.180
Next, the inventory.
